  "description": "q-Pochhammer denominator zeros at substrate q_0, paired against Picard-Lefschetz discriminant roots of the color-tube cubic. Provides the candidate Newton-Raphson seed values for the Thurston gluing equations on the active JSJ piece.  Arithmetic is mpmath end to end as of 2026-09-13: the discriminant's coefficients are exact integers, its derivative is taken analytically rather than by a 1e-7 central finite difference, and the roots come from mp.polyroots with extraprec instead of numpy.polyroots at 53 bits.  The per-depth newton_runs keep the historical loop and tol = 1e-12, so iterations and converged_at are directly comparable across the change; the precision-appropriate question (steps to tol = 1e-40) is asked separately in newton_deep_convergence.  MEASURED CONSEQUENCE: the float64 iterates were wrong from the first step — 2.27e-8 relative at residual_trace[1], 1.75e-5 by step 8, and 29 % on the final residual (2.998e-13 recorded against an exact 2.120e-13).  That is the 1e-7 central-difference derivative, not float64 rounding, and it means the convergence trace this probe exists to report carried ~8 correct digits at best.  The converged_at verdicts and iteration counts are unchanged, so the verdict at the historical tolerance survives — but the reading that k=3 FAILS to converge does not: with an exact derivative it reaches 1e-40 at iteration 23 (|D_*| = 4.84e-42), so its recorded converged_at:null is an artefact of max_iter = 16, not a mathematical fact.  A float64 run could not have decided that at any step count: at ~1e-8 iterate error the iteration stalls near 1e-13 and never reaches 1e-40.",
